.TH DKIM-RELAY 8 "April 2025" "dkim-relay" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dkim-relay \- SMTP server for DKIM signing and relaying email
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dkim-relay
[\-p pidfile] [\-c config_file] [\-l log_file] [\-d]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The dkim-relay program runs as an SMTP server which creates a
DKIM-Signature header for incoming email, then relays the email to another
SMTP server as configured. The user may use the included program
.B dkim-gen-keys
to create the RSA private key and a corresponding BIND zone file entry for the
public key. Keys are RSA up to 2048 bits; however, 1024-bit keys may be more
practical since many DNS servers can fragment responses that exceed a certain
size. Such fragmentation may cause issues with some DKIM verifiers.
.PP
Configuration is provided in a file (typically
.I config.toml
) where you can set parameters such as:
.RS
.TP
.B [server]
.IP "listen_host"
The host on which the SMTP server will listen.
.TP
.IP "listen_port"
The TCP port the server will bind to.
.TP
.IP "drop_user"
The unprivileged username to drop privileges to after startup.
.TP
.IP "tls_enabled"
Set to true to enable TLS.
.TP
.IP "cert_path"
Path to the server certificate (PEM format).
.TP
.IP "key_path"
Path to the server private key (PEM format).
.TP
.IP "auth_username" and "auth_password"
SMTP authentication credentials for incoming connections.
.RE
.PP
Also, configure each domain to be signed. For each domain you want DKIM
signing to be applied, add a configuration block as follows:
.PP
.EX
[[domain]]
name = "example.com"
selector = "selector"
private_key = """
-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
...
-----END PRIVATE KEY-----
"""
relay = "tls://smtp.example.com"
relay_port = 587
helo_host = "this.host.name"
relay_auth_user = "user"
relay_auth_password = "password"
.EX
.PP
After configuring your domains, ensure that the configuration file is owned by
root and has restrictive permissions (e.g. chmod 400 or 600) to prevent unintended
users from reading private keys and credentials.
.SH STARTING THE SERVER
The available command-line switches are:
.IP "\-p pidfile" 4
Path to a PID file.
.IP "\-c config_file" 4
Path to the configuration file.
.IP "\-l log_file" 4
Path to the log file.
.IP "\-d" 4
Daemonize the process.
.PP
For example, you might start the program with:
.P
\fB./target/release/dkim-relay \-p /var/run/dkim.pid \\
    \-c /root/dkim-relay/config.toml \-l /var/dkim/dkim.log \-d\fP
.SH INSTALLATION
To build the program, run:
.P
\fBcargo build --release\fP
.PP
For debugging purposes:
.P
\fBcargo build\fP
.SH HOW IT WORKS
.IP "1. SMTP CONNECTION & PARSING"
When a client connects to the server and sends SMTP commands, the email is
parsed into separate header and body structures. The server does not alter the
original email content, except by adding a DKIM-Signature header at the top of the
header block.
.IP "2. CANONICALIZATION"
The program uses relaxed/relaxed canonicalization, where:
.RS
.TP
Headers are transformed by:
- Converting the field name to lowercase.
- Removing any extra white space around the colon separator.
- Compressing sequences of whitespace in the header field value into a single SP.
.TP
Body is canonicalized by:
- Removing trailing white space on each line.
- Removing all trailing empty lines.
- Joining lines with CRLF, and including exactly one CRLF at the end (if not empty).
.RE
.IP "3. HASHING AND SIGNING"
A 32-byte SHA-256 hash is computed on the canonicalized body.
.PP
For the headers:
.RS
A temporary DKIM-Signature header is appended to the header block with its
B bh=
tag populated with the body hash, but the
B b=
tag left blank.
Then, the canonicalized header data (which includes the DKIM-Signature header with an empty
B b=
tag) is hashed.
.PP
This hash is combined with a fixed DER prefix for SHA-256 (19 bytes for SHA-256)
to produce a 51-byte digest, which is then signed using the RSA private key with
PKCS#1 v1.5 padding. The resulting signature is base64-encoded and inserted into
the final DKIM-Signature header.
.IP "4. HEADER LOCKING"
By optionally including blank headers in the canonicalized header data, the absence
of a header is “locked in.” This prevents a third party from inserting such headers later,
which would otherwise not break the signature.
.IP "5. HEADER PLACEMENT"
When the DKIM-Signature header is constructed (with the b= field populated),
it is placed at the top of the header block. If multiple DKIM-Signature headers
exist, the topmost one is used by verifiers.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP "1. PRIVILEGE DROPPING"
After reading the configuration and opening any privileged sockets (e.g. port 25),
the daemon will drop privileges to an unprivileged user (as specified in the config).
.IP "2. STARTTLS FOR INCOMING CONNECTIONS"
TLS is optional for incoming and outgoing connections. Currently, the server supports 
STARTTLS on outgoing connections but not (yet) on incoming connections. The incoming 
connection supports SSL (typically SSL SMTP runs on port 465, but can be configured 
to your preference).
